      "card_ids": [
        "C127", "C129", "C175", "C160", "C168", "C135", "C132", "C133",
        "C136", "C130", "C128", "C161", "C162", "C164", "C145", "C170",
        "C158", "C140", "C173"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule; all support is transferred from structurally related basic amines.",
    "L3 oral AUC/Cmax records lack IV comparators, so they confirm exposure but not the F fraction.",
    "No direct first-pass metabolism or clearance data for the query; F depends on both absorption and first-pass extraction.",
    "The query is predicted to be fully ionized (dicationic) at physiological pH; no direct permeability measurement quantifies the impact of the second positive charge.",
    "No context exactly matches the N-phenyl-N-indanyl propylenediamine scaffold."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "L4 intestinal absorption and permeability evidence for close analogs (imipramine 100% absorbed, desipramine high-permeability standard, amitriptyline/pheniramine/diphenhydramine high Caco-2 permeability) strengthens the absorption component for the query. Combined with direct human F values for similar lipophilic basic amines (diphenhydramine 0.69, urapidil 72–78%, donepezil ~100%, etc.) and L3 oral exposure records, the evidence supports F ≥ 20%. Low-F records are non-transferable polar-acid/prodrug or species-specific contexts. Prediction remains high; confidence increased to high."
}

